The IR (`KARAC_MIR_LL`) of the first case stores the call's `float` into the `double` slot's first 4 bytes, copies 4 bytes from an uninitialized alloca into the other half, and loads the slot as a `double`; no `fpext` (and for the int case no `sext`). So it is not only a wrong value but a read of uninitialized stack, which is why ASan's numbers differ.

Probably (inferred from the IR, not traced in the builder): `widen` in src/mir/lower.rs (design.md §5, widening implicit at a binding, an argument, a field or a return) is applied on the paths that work, but a call lowered straight into the destination place (the binding's local, the return place, the `if`'s result temp) skips it; the interpreter tolerates the type mismatch because its values carry their own type. `implicit_widening_at_known_destinations` (lower.rs) covers only a binding initialized from another binding and runs on the interpreter, which cannot see this.

Expected: the call writes a temp of its own return type and the destination gets `Cast(FloatToFloat | IntToInt, temp)`, as `widen` does elsewhere; and a test that runs these forms on mir-llvm. The app writes `f32_of(bits) as f64`; mini_read_stata.orig.kara keeps the first-written form.

