`karac check`: the error above, pointing at the capture `name` at 3:22. `karac __mir-run` and `__mir-llvm-run`: `4` then `abc`. Same with an iterator adapter (`h.iter().filter(|u| mask[*u % 2]).count()` then `let m2 = mask;`), with an indexing read or a method call in the body, and with the binding an `own` parameter moved into a struct literal afterwards (`BusCal { mask: mask, holidays: hs }`, the shape that hit it in the app). Not refused: reading the binding again after the closure instead of moving it, or `name.clone()` at the move.

Where it likely comes from (inferred, not bisected): the loop-of-consume rule in src/rc_predicate.rs (`loop_of_consume_candidates_from_sites`), which since B-2026-10-25-14 / B-2026-10-26-11 pairs closure-body uses with `capture_scope`; the closure body seems to count as a loop whose consume is the later move. Per core-semantics §9.3 a non-escaping closure borrows its captures, so the borrow ends at the closure's last use and the move after it is legal.

