2939cedca0. An auto-par loop whose body pushes onto `acc` exactly once per iteration now collects in place. The push must be a statement of the body itself, the element type must need no drop, and `acc` must be named nowhere else in the body. `acc` is reserved once for every push before any task is spawned (`Vec.__par_window`). Each task stores into its own window past `acc`'s length (`Vec.__par_put`) and returns how many it stored. The join counts the windows into `acc`'s length in iteration order (`Vec.__par_absorb`), so after a panic `acc` holds exactly the runs joined before it. A debug build of the compiler aborts if a task did not fill its window. A conditional push keeps the chunk-then-extend form, as the review thread narrowed it, and so does KARAC_AUTO_PAR_WINDOW=0. The invariants are in the comment on `Bx::push_window` (src/mir/lower.rs); there is no §11 text, since nothing observable changes.
INTERPRETER: tasks run at their spawn in iteration order, so its put is a push onto `acc` and its absorb does nothing. Only memory differs from the compiled form, and the interpreter already ran chunk-free.
MEASURED (Linux x86, kata 270 bench/bst_close, VmHWM through getrusage): 2 workers 20.9 MB against 37.5 MB chunked, 4 workers 20.6 against 37.5. Output identical, time unchanged (0.37/0.20 s at 2/4 workers). At 1 worker the build runs its in-order program, which is unchanged: 36.6 MB, the glibc realloc copy of a growing large Vec. The macOS figures in the detail (44-49 MiB) have not been re-measured here.
TESTS: tests/mir_auto_par_window.rs covers five cases:
- the in-place form's output against the in-order interpreter at 1, 2 and 4 workers, its task growing no Vec;
- a conditional push keeping the chunks;
- the peak of 2M i64s at one copy against the chunked two;
- ASan clean on a task panicking mid-run, at 1 and 4 workers;
- ASan clean on a cancelled task's collecting loop. A body that would suspend keeps its loops in order today (`suspends_as_value`), so the cancel path does not reach a window yet.
ASan and TSan corpus lanes: 54/54 each over the entries with an order-free push loop.

