}, unsafe {
    b
} };` and the second `unsafe` starts on the line `}, unsafe {`. The lint (src/unsafe_lint.rs check_unsafe_span) reads only the comment block directly above the line the `unsafe` starts on, so the statement's comment covers the first block and not the second. Any call with two unsafe arguments, `f(unsafe { a }, unsafe { b })`, has the same shape. Formatted code being refused where its source was not breaks the fmt round-trip rule; the canonical form can be silenced only by `#[allow(undocumented_unsafe)]` or by moving each block into its own statement. Remedy (a hypothesis): accept the comment block above the enclosing statement, as clippy's undocumented_unsafe_blocks does. The pin was rewritten with the unsafe call in a helper fn.

