name = "app"`; src/main.kara `struct V { x: i64 }
impl Add for V { type Output = V; fn add(self, o: V) -> V { V { x: self.x + o.x } } }
fn main() { let c = V { x: 1 } + V { x: 2 }; println(c.x); }`. `karac check`: error[E0108] at 2:6 with help `remove the impl block`. As a single file: prints 3. Same for an impl in a submodule used from main, and for `impl Index[(i64, i64)] for Grid` with `g[1, 2]` multi-index (single file prints 5). src/resolver/resolve_items.rs returns early only when `self.user_operator_impls` is set (B-2026-10-14-30), so the package resolver (cli::build_cmds::resolve_modules) evidently never sets it. design.md §6 Operators are trait calls, §9 Operator traits (`impl Add for Money`). The E0108 explain page (src/cli/explain.rs:1679) also still says operator traits are stdlib-only.

design.md § Coherence: two impls overlap when their headers unify, ignoring bounds. `W[A[T]]` and `W[B[T]]` do not unify (A != B), yet the typechecker's overlap check (src/typechecker/env_build.rs impl_overlap_conflict) keys a generic impl on its target's NAME: an impl whose target args are not fully concrete stores no `target_args`, so any two generic impls of one trait for `W` collide. Repro: `struct A[T] { x: T }
struct B[T] { x: T }
struct W[I] { it: I }
trait Name { fn name(ref self) -> String; }
impl[T] Name for W[A[T]] { fn name(ref self) -> String { "a" } }
impl[T] Name for W[B[T]] { fn name(ref self) -> String { "b" } }
fn main() { println(W { it: A { x: 1 } }.name()); println(W { it: B { x: 1 } }.name()); }` -> `error[typecheck]: 6:1: conflicting impl: `W` already implements `Name`; a type implements a trait once, and two impls whose headers unify overlap whatever their bounds` and `no method 'name' on type 'W'`. Expected `a`, `b`. In runtime/stdlib/source the same shape is not reported: the second impl is skipped, so `DoubleEndedIterator for IterStepBy[Range[T]]` plus `...[RangeInclusive[T]]` left `(0..=9).step_by(3).next_back()` as `no method 'next_back'`. B-2026-11-05-35 worked around it with one impl over a private `StepBack` trait (runtime/stdlib/source/range.kara), to be replaced by the two direct impls once this is fixed. A fix also needs method lookup (find_impl's first match by name) to choose the impl whose written shape fits the receiver, which impl_shape_fits already does for find_method_with_args.

design.md §4 Conditional compilation: `#[cfg]` (interim spelling `#[target(..)]`, same rules) may appear on items, impl members, struct fields and enum variants; a declaration whose condition does not hold does not exist on that target. At 760d8f9ec: (1) `struct S {}
impl S {
#[target(native)]
fn f(self) -> i64 { 1 }
#[target(wasm_wasi)]
fn f(self) -> i64 { 2 }
}
fn main() { println(S {}.f()); }` passes check and prints 2 on run, __mir-run, __mir-llvm-run and ASan (expected 1): the later member silently replaces the native one, a wrong value. (2) `struct S { a: i64, #[target(wasm_wasi)] b: i64 }` with `S { a: 1 }` -> `missing field 'b' in struct 'S'`. (3) `enum E { A, #[target(wasm_wasi)] B }` with `match e { E.A => .. }` -> `non-exhaustive match: missing variants: B`. Item-level gating works: native/wasm twin fns and consts pick the native one, a use of a wasm-only fn or struct is refused naming the target (also across package imports), two #[target] attributes and an unknown target are refused, #[target] on a statement is refused, and `karac check` checks each target's declarations.

design.md §9 `Copy`, `Clone` and `Drop`: Drop and Copy are exclusive; the compiler rejects the combination at the impl or derive, naming both traits, because a destructor that runs on every bitwise copy releases the resource twice. At 760d8f9ec: `#[derive(Copy, Clone)]
struct Pt { x: i64 }
impl Drop for Pt { fn drop(mut ref self) { println(f"drop {self.x}"); } }
fn take(p: own Pt) { println(f"took {p.x}"); }
fn main() { let a = Pt { x: 1 }; let b = a; take(a); take(b); println("end"); }` passes check and prints `took 1 / drop 1 / took 1 / drop 1 / end / drop 1 / drop 1` on run, __mir-run and ASan: one constructed value is dropped four times. With a drop that closes a descriptor or frees an FFI buffer this is a double release. The manual `impl Copy` is refused correctly (only the derive path is open).

At 760d8f9ec on every lane. (1) design.md §9 Derive/Default: on `Pair[T]` the derive generates `impl[T: Default] Default for Pair[T]`. `#[derive(Default)]
struct Pair[T] { a: T }
fn main() { let q: Pair[i64] = Pair.default(); println(q.a); }` -> `cannot #[derive(Default)] for 'Pair' — field 'a' of type 'T' does not implement 'Default'` plus `no associated function 'default' on type 'Pair'` (the non-generic `Point` derive works). (2) §9 Operator traits: PartialEq and PartialOrd take Rhs so a cross-type comparison is an impl. `struct Meters { v: i64 }
impl PartialEq[i64] for Meters { fn eq(self, o: ref i64) -> bool { self.v == *o } }` then `Meters { v: 3 } == 3` -> `parameter 'o' of impl method 'Meters.eq' is i64, but trait 'PartialEq' declares Meters there` and `cannot compare 'Meters' and 'i64'` (with a by-value `o: i64` it also reports a mode mismatch). (3) §9 Eq/Ord/Hash: the derive generates `self.a.hash(mut hasher)` per field and a manual impl may do the same. `impl Hash for Ci { fn hash[H: Hasher](self, hasher: mut ref H) { self.s.to_lowercase().hash(hasher); } }` -> `hash takes a Hasher, but H is not one`; calling `hasher.write_i64(..)` directly works, and a Set over that impl dedups correctly, so only field-to-hasher composition fails.

design.md §12 Trait methods and generic calls: a trait method's clause is a ceiling; impls may narrow, never widen. §12 Default-permitted effects: allocates(Heap) and panics never have to be declared, and a function type's clause that omits them still accepts a value that allocates or panics; §12's own example `impl Storage for ReadOnlyView { .. fn save(self, key: Key, value: Value) with writes(Data) { panic("read-only view"); } }` is marked OK. At 760d8f9ec: (1) `effect resource Data;
trait Storage { fn save(self, k: i64) with writes(Data); }
struct Ro {}
impl Storage for Ro { fn save(self, k: i64) with writes(Data) { panic("read-only"); } }` -> error[effect] `impl method 'Ro.save' performs panics() but trait 'Storage.save' ceiling is [writes(Data)]`; a body `let v: Vec[i64] = [k]; ..` adds `performs allocates(Heap) but ... ceiling`. Only `#[no_effect]` may forbid these. (2) `trait Storage { fn load(self, k: i64) -> i64 with reads(Data); }
