`karac run`, `KARAC_THREADS=1 karac run` and `karac __mir-run` all time out (20 s, rc 124) on a 4-core container. core-semantics.md §11.1: a task is guaranteed to let its siblings run at a suspension point, and a sleep is one (§11.8 lists a timer).

Cause: `__ready("sleep_ms", ms)` sets the deadline when first asked and reports ready as soon as it has passed. A zero (or negative) pause has passed on the first ask, so the call completes without parking and the task never leaves its worker. On the interpreter (`ready` in src/mir/interp.rs) the executor only takes another task when the running one parks or yields; natively `karac_mir_ready_sleep` (runtime/src/mir_task.rs) answers 1 the same way. On the pool the spinners each hold a worker, so once there are as many as workers the setter never runs.

Found by kata 1115 (Print FooBar Alternately), whose natural arm is the Python solution's two semaphores, `foo_sem = Semaphore.new(1)` and `bar_sem = Semaphore.new(0)`, each method acquiring its own and releasing the other's:

```kara
sync struct FooBar { n: i64, foo_sem: Semaphore, bar_sem: Semaphore }
// foo: for _ in 0..n { let _ = self.foo_sem.acquire(60000); print_foo(); self.bar_sem.release(); }
// bar: the same with the two swapped
```

Under a `TaskGroup` with one task each, n = 2 prints `foofoobarbar` (every backend; 85 of 2000 random runs alternate). Both halves are as the file note in runtime/stdlib/semaphore.kara describes: the interpreter's `semaphore_method` (src/mir/interp.rs, "One task runs at a time here, so an exhausted semaphore times out at once") and the runtime's `karac_runtime_semaphore_acquire` (runtime/src/semaphore.rs) never park, and `release` saturates at the starting count. The note dates the waiting to "once the event-loop backend lands"; the executor and `TaskGroup` have landed, and `acquire` is not among the suspending natives (`__ready`).

The saturation is a design choice the stdlib note gives a reason for (a release past the budget is a bookkeeping bug), but it rules out the signaling use every other language's counting semaphore has (Python `threading.Semaphore`, POSIX `sem_post`, Tokio `add_permits`); a bounded variant is usually a separate type (`BoundedSemaphore`). The kata's semaphore arm uses a hand-built one (a `channel` holding the permits) meanwhile.

